k for cmd/unexpand since it's a new package, even though cmd/ binaries don't really export anything - the contract would just define the main function signature and any internal helper functions. But I'm also constrained to 4 tasks total, so I need to be strategic about what I include. I'm realizing expand already has implementations for R1.1-R2.4, so no contract is needed there. For unexpand as a new package, I need to create the contract first before implementation, which means I can fit four tasks: completing expand with R3.1-R4.3, then starting unexpand with its contract and initial requirements R1.1-R1.4. Now I need to verify the weight distribution works with this plan. I need to pull the SRD files to get the actual requirement details, since I only have the IDs and states in my context. I should structure the task descriptions to direct the stitch agent to read those SRDs for specifics, and I'll follow the naming pattern from the previous expand tasks like \"Task 5268: [stitch] cmd/expand: implement srd024-expand R2.1-R2.4\".\n\nFor the unexpand contract task, I'm thinking about what the stub structure should look like—cmd packages don't export anything, so the \"contract\" really means laying out the main.go structure with placeholder functions that the implementation tasks can fill in. main function with flag parsing and core logic\n- <name>_test.go: differential tests\n\nThe \"contract\" for a cmd/ package is tricky since there are no exports. But I should follow the constraint and propose one anyway—it would define the main function skeleton and internal helper signatures.\n\nActually, rereading the constraint: \"Before proposing implementation tasks for a new package, propose a contract task that defines all exported types, interfaces, and function signatures as Go stubs.\" For a cmd/ binary with no exports, the contract would just be an empty main.go with package declaration and import stubs, which feels like a waste of one of my four task slots.\n\nBut